  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\nIssued at 134 AM CDT WED JUL 22 2026\n\nGuidance and NHC tract continue to keep Tropical Storm Bertha\nmoving to the west and hugging the Louisiana Gulf Coast today and\nThursday before likely making landfall along the Southeast Texas\nCoast Thursday night. Impacts from Bertha`s westward track for \nCentral Alabama will be southeast to south-southeast winds \nslightly elevated with a few minor gusts, but under wind advisory \nfor today and convection across mainly the southeast half of the \narea. At the same time, upper high pressure will be situated from \nColorado to West Texas to Oklahoma. Northwest upper flow will \nresult across the Dakotas and Upper Plains from being wedged \nbetween the ridge and an Eastern Canada low. Also over Eastern \nCanada will be a surface low moving eastward that will drag a \nfront across New England and into the Deep South including \nNorthern Alabama by late today with favorable assistance from the \nupper northwest flow. However, this front is expected to stall \nacross the middle part of Alabama Thursday. It is expected to \nremain across Central Alabama through late Friday into early \nSaturday before pushing back northward as a warm front. This will \nkeep the area in a wet pattern more so after Bertha departs, into \nthis weekend.\n\nWhile most of the convection from Bertha is along the southeast\nsections, hot and humid air will continue to allow for heat\nindices to approach up to the 103-106 range for the west sections\nfor one more day today again. A heat advisory remains in effect.\nThe good news is that increased rain chances and a surface front\nmoving into the area will lower temperatures some for Thursday\ninto this weekend which should keep us just below heat advisory\ncriteria. However, the reprieve will be short lived. Over the\nweekend, the upper ridge slowly expands/nudges eastward. This\nwill result in hotter temperatures returning back to Central\nAlabama and only isolated diurnal convection. This also means a\nmedium to high chance that heat advisories will be needed again\nby next Monday and Tuesday for portions of the area.\n\n08",
